WEBVTT

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:08.943
In this lesson, you install Rosetta DBT Studio, the one app we use for the whole course.

00:00:09.223 --> 00:00:15.600
You download it, copy it to your computer, and its first-run setup installs Python and dbt Core for you.

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:20.534
Studio is open source, and every version is published on GitHub.

00:00:20.814 --> 00:00:24.809
Open the releases page of rosettadb, dbt-studio.

00:00:25.089 --> 00:00:27.785
The latest release is 1.7.6.

00:00:28.735 --> 00:00:30.098
Scroll down to Assets.

00:00:30.378 --> 00:00:39.267
There is a build for every system: a dmg for Mac, an exe for Windows, and deb, rpm and flatpak packages for Linux.

00:00:40.217 --> 00:00:47.303
On a Mac, pick the dmg for your processor: arm64 for Apple silicon, x64 for Intel.

00:00:47.583 --> 00:00:51.442
This Mac has an Intel chip, so click the x64 dmg.

00:00:52.392 --> 00:00:54.632
When the download finishes, open the file.

00:00:54.912 --> 00:00:59.088
A window shows the app on the left, and your Applications folder on the right.

00:01:00.038 --> 00:01:05.195
Drag Rosetta DBT Studio onto Applications, and wait for the copy to finish.

00:01:06.145 --> 00:01:07.967
Then open it from Applications.

00:01:08.247 --> 00:01:13.415
The first time, macOS asks whether you want to open an app downloaded from the internet.

00:01:13.695 --> 00:01:14.478
Click Open.

00:01:15.428 --> 00:01:17.178
Studio starts with a short setup.

00:01:17.458 --> 00:01:19.309
First, the dbt version.

00:01:19.589 --> 00:01:22.664
Choose dbt 1.x, the recommended one.

00:01:23.614 --> 00:01:24.886
Then the exact version.

00:01:25.166 --> 00:01:27.765
Keep the latest, 1.12.6.

00:01:28.715 --> 00:01:32.903
Next, the adapters, one for each database dbt can talk to.

00:01:33.183 --> 00:01:39.308
This course only needs DuckDB, so uncheck the others, keep dbt-duckdb, and click Continue.

00:01:40.258 --> 00:01:42.591
Skip the optional tools, and click Install.

00:01:43.541 --> 00:01:51.869
Studio downloads its own copy of Python, then installs dbt Core and the DuckDB adapter, all inside its own folder.

00:01:52.149 --> 00:01:55.245
Here it took about a minute and a half; the video speeds it up.

00:01:56.195 --> 00:02:02.759
Python 3.10.17, and dbt 1.12.6 with one adapter.

00:02:03.039 --> 00:02:03.797
Click Finish.

00:02:04.747 --> 00:02:07.603
This is Studio's home screen, the Workspace Overview.

00:02:07.883 --> 00:02:11.109
No projects yet: we create the first one in Module 2.

00:02:12.059 --> 00:02:21.985
The status bar at the bottom shows what was installed: dbt 1.12.6, Python 3.10.17, and DuckDB.

00:02:22.935 --> 00:02:26.599
At the top right are New Connection, Open Project, and Settings.

00:02:26.879 --> 00:02:28.209
That is all we need for now.

00:02:29.159 --> 00:02:31.011
To recap: you installed one app.

00:02:31.291 --> 00:02:37.676
Studio set up its own Python and dbt Core, with the DuckDB adapter, inside its own folder.

00:02:37.956 --> 00:02:40.939
You did not install Python or dbt yourself.

00:02:41.889 --> 00:02:47.320
Next, we create the dbt project, and connect it to a repository on GitHub.
